    <h2>Sizing Is the Whole Ballgame</h2>

    <p>Ask a Hartselle homeowner what they shopped for on their last system and they will name the brand and the SEER rating. Ask what almost nobody checked and it is the one thing that decides whether the system actually works: was it sized with a Manual J load calculation, or did the installer just match the tonnage of the old unit? Matching the old unit is the industry's default shortcut, and it is wrong more often than it is right — a lot of the systems already in these homes were oversized when they went in, so matching them just repeats a decade-old mistake at today's prices.</p>

    <p>Here is what goes wrong when a system is oversized, and it is the most common comfort complaint in this market: a 4-ton unit dropped on a house that only needs 3 tons cools the air to the thermostat setpoint fast and shuts off before it has run long enough to pull the humidity out. You get a house that is cold and clammy at the same time, an AC that clicks on and off every few minutes — short-cycling — and a compressor wearing out years early from all that starting and stopping. A right-sized system runs longer, gentler cycles, holds the humidity down, and lasts. The Manual J is not paperwork for its own sake; it is the whole difference between those two outcomes.</p>

    <h2>How to Tell a Real Installer From a Box-Swapper</h2>

    <p>So how does a homeowner spot the competent installer instead of the one who is just going to swap a box? Ask for the load calculation in writing. A real installer hands you a Manual J with room-by-room numbers; a box-swapper says "you've got a 4-ton now, we'll drop in a 4-ton" and changes the subject. Ask what they are doing about the ductwork, too — on a lot of Hartselle homes the ducts leak 20 to 30 percent of the conditioned air into the attic and crawlspace, and a new high-efficiency unit bolted onto leaky ducts never delivers the efficiency you paid for. A real quote includes sealing, and where the numbers call for it, resizing the return-air path so the blower can actually breathe.</p>

    <p>The honest admission most homeowners never hear: two written quotes for the same house can land thousands of dollars apart, and the cheaper one is sometimes the worse deal. The low bid usually got there by skipping the load calc and the duct work — the invisible parts — and selling you the same box for less. You find that out three summers later, when the house still will not hold 72 on a July afternoon. The bidder worth trusting is often not the cheapest; it is the one who actually measured before quoting.</p>

    <h2>The R-22 Question and When Replacement Makes Sense</h2>

    <p>The other decision homeowners wrestle with is when to stop repairing and replace. The rough rule holds: past 15 years, once a repair crosses about a third of replacement cost, you are throwing good money after bad. The sharper trigger here is refrigerant. Systems installed in the late 1990s and 2000s — plenty of them, in the older homes near downtown and in the first wave of subdivisions like Tanner Heights alike — often still run R-22, which has been phased out of production. Topping off a leaky R-22 system now costs more per pound than the recharge is worth on a unit already near the end of its life. A failed compressor or a leaking evaporator coil on a 15-plus-year R-22 system is the signal to replace rather than pour money into it.</p>

    <p>When you do replace, sizing still rules the decision, but efficiency is where you get a real choice. Federal minimum in the southern region is 14.3 SEER2 for a split system, and for most Hartselle homes a 15-to-16 SEER2 unit is the sweet spot. A high-end 18-plus SEER2 variable-speed system earns its premium mainly in a larger home run hard year-round — the kind of custom build you find out in Bethel Ridge — while on a modest 1,800-square-foot house the utility savings take many years to pay back the extra cost. Buying more SEER than your house can use is just another way to overspend; buying the right size is where the money is actually saved.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What HVAC Work Costs in Hartselle</h2>

    <p>Installed ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Service call / no-cool diagnostic</strong> — about $85 to $150 to show up and find the fault.</li>
      <li><strong>2.5- to 3-ton system replacement</strong> — roughly $7,000 to $11,000 for a standard swap in an existing home.</li>
      <li><strong>4- to 5-ton or ductwork-reworked install</strong> — about $12,000 to $18,000, with the ducts and line set, not the box, driving the number.</li>
      <li><strong>Full custom install with zoning</strong> — a larger new-construction system with proper zoning can run higher still; get the Manual J in writing.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The honest admission most homeowners never hear: two quotes for the same house can be thousands apart and the cheaper one is sometimes the worse deal, because it skipped the load calc and the duct sealing that make the system last. Ask both bidders for the Manual J.</p>

    <h2>When to Do It</h2>

    <p>Replace on your schedule, not the system's. Spring and fall are the smart windows — installers are less slammed than during a July heat wave, and you are not making a rushed five-figure decision while your family sweats. That rush is a bad spot for anyone, and worse for the commuter family that drives I-65 to Cummings Research Park or Decatur every day and has no time to babysit a failing system. If your unit is over 15 years old and still on R-22, treat the next significant failure as the moment to plan the replacement rather than sink another repair into it.</p>

    <h2>Questions Hartselle Homeowners Ask</h2>

    <p><strong>Repair or replace?</strong></p>
    <p>Past 15 years with a repair over a third of replacement cost, replace — especially an R-22 system with a failed compressor or coil.</p>

    <p><strong>Why is my house humid with the AC on?</strong></p>
    <p>Almost always an oversized unit that short-cycles before it can dehumidify. It comes from skipping the Manual J load calc.</p>

    <p><strong>What SEER should I buy?</strong></p>
    <p>15 to 16 SEER2 fits most Hartselle homes. High-end variable-speed pays off mainly in a large home run hard year-round.</p>

    <p><strong>What does it cost?</strong></p>
    <p>Roughly $7,000 to $11,000 for a standard swap, $12,000 to $18,000 with ductwork or a bigger system.</p>

